  <p>The grid leaves two things open: how often to post, and when.</p>

  <p>The networks' own pages, read for this guide, give no number for either.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"You don't need to post daily, but creating a content schedule and sticking to it will help you build momentum."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok for Business, creative strategy for small businesses, updated September 2026, the network's own guidance, with no figure attached.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Meta's planner suggests posting times from when your followers were most active on Facebook and Instagram over the past 7 days.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Business Help Center, creating posts in Meta Business Suite, read October 11, 2026, a description of the network's own tool.</p>

  <p>Pick a cadence your team can keep for a month, and let your own audience data set the hours. <a href="/resources/insights/holiday-content-calendar-2026">The holiday content calendar 2026</a> shows how far ahead the big retail dates need their assets.</p>

  <h2>Plan: how do you build the week from one campaign?</h2>

  <p>Open Campaigns and click New campaign. Give it a name and a brief: the goal, the audience, the key message, the dates. Then add the files the week will draw on, from the product shots to last month's best clip.</p>

  <p>A campaign points at files in the Assets Library and copies nothing, so one picture can sit in several campaigns. While a campaign is picked in the Campaign choice of a studio or a post, everything that page saves joins it. By the end of the afternoon, the whole week sits under one name. <a href="/app/campaigns">The app's Campaigns page</a> shows the rest of what a campaign holds.</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Campaign brief</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Launch week for our new rain jacket, Monday to Friday. Audience: commuters and weekend hikers who already own one rain jacket and don't trust it. Key message: taped seams, packs into its own pocket. Tone: plain, a little dry. Every post points to the product page. Avoid "revolutionary" and anything about the planet we can't prove.</p>
  </div>

  <p>The brief pays off twice. A LinkedIn, Facebook or X post can be drafted from the whole campaign: pick it under Skills and material, in Context folder from the Assets Library, then Draft with AI. hubStudio reads the brief, the list of files (each name and type, and the prompt behind a render) and the text of up to 20 documents. It never looks at the pictures themselves. Say in the brief what they show.</p>

  <h2>Make: how do you write the posts and make the pictures?</h2>

  <p>Each network has its own module, and a post moves through it in steps, from the brief or the visual to publishing.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/Images/help/linkedin-brief.webp" alt="The LinkedIn module on a new post: the band with All posts, New post and the four step tiles, and the brief with Format, Emoticons, Language, the model picker on Balanced, Draft with AI and Write it myself" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>A LinkedIn post starts from its brief: pick the format, then Draft with AI or Write it myself.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>On LinkedIn, Facebook and X you start from words. Pick the format (text only, an image or a carousel), the language and the model, write the brief, then press Draft with AI. Or press Write it myself, which opens the editor with no AI call and nothing billed.</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Post brief, LinkedIn</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Founder's note for launch day. Two short paragraphs on why we spent a year on seams nobody sees. First line has to work before "see more". End on the product page, no hashtags in the body, three at the end.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Instagram and TikTok start from the visual. On Instagram you pick One image, Carousel or Reel first; TikTok takes a video.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/Images/help/instagram-picture.webp" alt="The Instagram module on a new post: the band with the three step tiles, and the picture or the video step with One image, Carousel and Reel, Render with AI, Pick from the library and Upload from your computer, with the Skills and material strip unfolded beside it" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>On Instagram the visual comes first: render it, pick it from the library or upload it.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The pictures come three ways: Render with AI inside the post, Pick from the library, or Upload from your computer. A rendered carousel runs 2 to 8 slides. The price sits on the render button before you press it. A picture takes a minute or two (a clip, a few minutes). Start the next post while it works.</p>

  <p>Two tools help with the visuals. The Image editor, which is free, crops a picture to the network's format and checks it before you save. Shorts autopilot cuts one long interview or talk into vertical clips, captioned, with a caption for each network, ready to pick in the TikTok or Instagram module.</p>

  <h2>Adapt: how do you turn one post into posts for five networks?</h2>

  <p>Finish the strongest post first. Then, at the foot of its publishing step, open Re-purpose for other networks, tick the networks and pick a way:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Manually.</strong> Each network opens in its own tab with this post as the brief. Nothing is drafted until you press Draft there.</li>
    <li><strong>Draft at once.</strong> hubStudio drafts every network's version and opens each in a tab. Each draft is billed like one Draft with AI.</li>
    <li><strong>Publish automatically.</strong> The same drafts, sent on the accounts you tick. YouTube can't be ticked here.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A network that can't take the post is grayed out: Instagram needs a picture, TikTok and YouTube a video. Each draft is shaped by that network's own format skill, picked by default, which carries its posting rules on length, hashtags and tone, so each version is rewritten rather than pasted.</p>

  <p>The limits still differ from one network to the next.</p>

