  <blockquote class="evidence">Instagram captions take up to 2,200 characters, 30 hashtags and 20 @ tags; TikTok video captions up to 2,200; LinkedIn posts up to 3,000; X posts up to 280, with emoji counting as two.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Instagram and TikTok developer references, LinkedIn Help and X's developer documentation, read October 11, 2026, each network's own page.</p>

  <p>Every limit on this page was read on the network's own page on October 11, 2026. Networks change them with little notice. Check again before a big batch.</p>

  <p>Two networks also cap how much one account can post in a day through an app.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Instagram's developer guide allows 100 API-published posts in a 24-hour moving period, a carousel counting as one. The same guide's carousel section says 50, and the quota endpoint reports a total of 50.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Instagram Platform developer documentation, Content Publishing guide and content publishing limit reference, read October 11, 2026, the platform's own API pages.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">TikTok caps posting through apps at "typically around 15 posts per day" per creator account, shared across every app that posts for it.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok for Developers, Content Sharing Guidelines, updated August 4, 2026, the platform's own developer rules.</p>

  <p>Both caps count per account, and a brand week sits far below them. An account that posts at volume should plan to the lower Instagram figure. <a href="/resources/insights/instagram-post-sizes-2026">The Instagram post sizes for 2026 guide</a> covers the picture shapes.</p>

  <h2>Approve and schedule: how do you get sign-off and load the queue?</h2>

  <p>Approval is the slow part. Send everything at once. On each post's publishing step, Send for validation names a teammate, or one of the client's people when the post is made for a client. The validator gets an email, then validates or sends it back with a comment.</p>

  <p>While it waits, the post is locked: nothing on it can be changed, deleted or published until they decide. Sent back, it returns to draft, and the next version stays on the same thread. A post tagged Made for a client also shows in that client's Client space.</p>

  <p>Once a post is approved, stay on the Publish automatically tab, tick the accounts and click Schedule. Pick a day and a time, or a chip: In an hour, Tonight, 18:00, Tomorrow, 09:00 or Monday, 09:00. Then Schedule it.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The time is read in your own time zone, set in User Settings.</li>
    <li>A post can go out at least a couple of minutes ahead and up to a year away.</li>
    <li>One post can go to up to 20 accounts, or 20 pages on Facebook.</li>
    <li>hubStudio checks the queue every five minutes, so 09:00 means 09:00 to 09:05.</li>
    <li>A temporary problem on the network's side is retried, three attempts in all. A content problem is not, and whoever queued the post gets an email saying what the network said.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>YouTube is the exception. YouTube keeps every upload from an app it hasn't audited private, so hubStudio prepares the video, its title and its description, and Publish interactively hands them to YouTube Studio. You pick Public or Schedule there.</p>

  <p><a href="/app/publish">The publish page of the app</a> sets out what each network takes.</p>

  <h2>What should you check before the week goes out?</h2>

  <p>Run this list once, at the end of the afternoon.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Every post carries one idea from the campaign brief, in its network's own words.</li>
    <li>Captions sit inside each network's limit, and X copy that runs long is set to go as a thread.</li>
    <li>Every picture is in the network's shape. Instagram posts need one, and TikTok and YouTube posts need their video.</li>
    <li>Every post is validated, or waiting with a named validator and a date.</li>
    <li>Scheduled times read right in your own time zone.</li>
    <li>Check the daily caps if one account carries many posts.</li>
    <li>YouTube uploads are in your own calendar, since hubStudio doesn't send them.</li>
    <li>Next week starts faster if every file the week used sits in the campaign.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If the team would rather hand over the making, <a href="/services/design/social-media">the social media design service</a> is the studio producing the same week to a brief. <a href="/solutions/platforms/linkedin">The LinkedIn platform page</a> covers what the studio makes for LinkedIn, and <a href="/resources/how-to">the how-to guides</a> carry the other workflows.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <h3>How do I plan a week of social media posts?</h3>

  <p>Start from one campaign brief and its files. Lay the week out as a grid of day, network, idea, visual and status, then work in four blocks: make the posts and their pictures, adapt each one for the other networks, send them for approval, and schedule them. Doing each block for the whole week at once is what makes it fit in an afternoon.</p>

  <h3>How far in advance can I schedule social posts?</h3>

  <p>In hubStudio, a post can be scheduled from a couple of minutes ahead to a year away, read in your own time zone. The queue is checked every five minutes, so a post set for 09:00 goes out by 09:05. Scheduling works on LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook, TikTok (Beta) and X. YouTube videos are scheduled by you in YouTube Studio.</p>

  <h3>How do I turn one post into posts for every network?</h3>

  <p>Finish one post, then open Re-purpose for other networks and tick the others. Manually opens each network with the post as its brief; Draft at once writes every version for you; Publish automatically drafts and sends them. Each version follows its network's own format rules, and a network that can't take the post, such as Instagram without a picture, is grayed out.</p>

  <h3>How many posts a day can I publish on Instagram?</h3>

  <p>It depends which of Meta's pages you read. Instagram's developer guide allows 100 posts through an app in a rolling 24 hours, a carousel counting as one, while the same guide's carousel section and its quota endpoint say 50. Plan to 50 per account. A normal week of brand posts never comes close.</p>

  <h3>Can a client approve posts before they are scheduled?</h3>

  <p>Yes. On the publishing step, Send for validation can name one of the client's people when the post is made for that client. They get an email, open the post, and validate it or send it back with a comment. Until they decide, the post is locked and can't be published, changed or deleted.</p>

  <h3>Can I schedule YouTube videos from the same place?</h3>

  <p>Not inside hubStudio. YouTube keeps uploads from apps it hasn't audited private, so hubStudio prepares the video, a title and a description written to YouTube's rules, then Publish interactively opens YouTube Studio with them ready. You pick Public or Schedule there. The other five networks are scheduled from hubStudio itself.</p>
